From c5b504d1dcdd16d7a69b685f9fb9a53c444d848d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mira <mira@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 31 May 2026 22:54:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] (be) snapshot-remote-unwind handler snapshot +
 coordinator remote unwind
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The handler runs NO libunwind. It captures the interrupted thread's
ucontext_t and a bounded slice of its stack into a per-sequence slot
and returns. The coordinator reads the slot after the handler exits
and runs libunwind in REMOTE mode (`unw_create_addr_space` +
`unw_init_remote`) against the snapshot, in the request thread,
where DWARF unwind info lookups via `dl_iterate_phdr` are safe.

This is the variant's defining structural difference from ck-phdr-
unwind and ob-kill60 (both run libunwind inside the handler). It
trades a larger handler payload (snapshot bytes) for a simpler
async-signal-safety profile in the handler — the handler does only
errno save/restore, a SI_QUEUE + getpid sender check, the per-
sequence seq+state CAS dance, a structure copy of ucontext_t, and
a bounded memcpy of stack bytes (guarded by mincore on the first
page and per-page within the cap).

The handler is marked `__attribute__((no_sanitize("address")))`
because under ASAN the per-thread stack contains compiler-inserted
poison redzones between the target thread's local variables. The
ASAN memcpy interceptor would report every cross-redzone read as
a stack-buffer-underflow even though the bytes are real and the
read is intentional (the snapshot is fed to libunwind later, which
chases CFI through the captured words). The annotation disables
ASAN instrumentation of THIS function only; live process code
outside the handler stays fully instrumented.

The slot ring + sequence + state machine mirror fp-walk / ck-phdr-
unwind / ob-kill60 so the late-handler safety contract is the same:
a coordinator can only re-arm a slot in IDLE, so a slot held by a
late WALKING handler is never re-armed under it. The codex-driven
slot-disarm fix shared across the three sibling variants (commit
2a00713 on phase2-spec-restructure) is mirrored verbatim:
`expected_seq` is stored to 0 BEFORE the state is stored to
SLOT_IDLE on both the success and timeout paths.

The remote-mode `unw_accessors_t` is implemented in-TU:
- `access_reg` reads from the captured ucontext_t (passed as
  `arg` to `unw_init_remote`); the regnum-to-gregs[] mapping
  follows the libunwind upstream UNW_X86_64_R<N> -> REG_R<N>
  convention.
- `access_mem` serves stack words from the snapshot if the
  address is in the captured window, else falls through to a
  direct in-process read of our own DSO text/eh_frame/data
  (mincore-guarded). The slot pointer is handed to access_mem
  via a thread-local set by the coordinator immediately before
  `unw_init_remote`.
- `find_proc_info` / `put_unwind_info` delegate to the
  exported `_Ux86_64_dwarf_find_proc_info` /
  `_Ux86_64_dwarf_put_unwind_info` helpers (declared via
  extern "C" in-TU because the public headers do not expose
  them but the archive does). Those helpers call our
  `access_mem` and walk `dl_iterate_phdr`, both safe in the
  coordinator context.
- `get_dyn_info_list_addr` returns 0 (Doris does not JIT
  eh_frame entries).
- `get_proc_name` returns UNW_ENOINFO (offline symbolization).
- `resume` is unused; returns -UNW_EINVAL.

A defense-in-depth `hasPHDRCache()` check refuses to signal if the
cache is missing. In this variant the cache matters for the
COORDINATOR's `unw_step` (not the handler), but the symptom of
a missing cache is the same — the libc `dl_iterate_phdr` takes
the loader lock and serializes the whole many-thread dump. The
cache is populated by the test runner's `updatePHDRCache()` call
before RUN_ALL_TESTS and by doris_main after MemInfo::init
(patch 2), so this is a guard, not the normal path.

Reference: libunwind tests/test-static-link-gen.c (the in-process
remote-unwind pattern: custom accessors, dwarf_search_unwind_table
for find_proc_info, direct memory reads); thirdparty
libunwind-coredump.a's `_UCD_*` accessors are the same shape
applied to a real coredump.
